feat(mcp): create tools from forwarded MCP definitions (#25185)

## Background

When browser WebMCP tools execute on the client while the model runs on
the backend, the API route receives serializable MCP definitions.
Previously each route had to validate and map them to
`dynamicTool`/`jsonSchema` manually; `client.toolsFromDefinitions()`
requires an existing connection and adds backend execute functions.

This is **PR 2 of 4**, stacked on #25184 and targeting `aayush/234`. The
final PR, #25186, adds the full browser/backend example.

## Summary

- Export `createMCPTools(definitions)` from `@ai-sdk/mcp` to validate
definitions and build dynamic tools without execute functions or an MCP
connection.
- Reuse schema conversion, titles, MCP annotations/app metadata, and
model-output conversion with the connected client; reject malformed
definitions and duplicate names while preserving prototype-named tools
safely.
- Add focused tests and public API docs; extend the existing changeset
from the transport PR.
- Wrap conversion failures, including malformed nested and legacy MCP
App resource URIs, in `MCPClientError` with the original error as
`cause`, so API routes can consistently reject invalid definitions with
HTTP 400.

Validation: 665 MCP tests passed in both Node and Edge; local package
build, `pnpm type-check:full`, formatting/linting, and documentation
validation passed.

## End-to-End Verification

Verified the backend handoff using definitions and results produced by
native Chrome WebMCP against the local built packages. `createMCPTools`
produced `getCart` without an execute function, and
`convertToModelMessages(..., { tools })` converted the returned MCP
result into text model content containing the $75 cart total. The
complete model/browser conversation is exercised in #25186.

<details>
<summary>Backend handoff verification</summary>

Build with `pnpm exec turbo build --filter=@ai-sdk/mcp...
--filter=ai...`. Run the native browser script in #25184 and save its
returned `{ definitions, first, second }` JSON as
`/tmp/ai-sdk-webmcp-native-result.json`. Save this script to
`/tmp/ai-sdk-webmcp-helper-check.mjs` and run `node --input-type=module
< /tmp/ai-sdk-webmcp-helper-check.mjs` from the repository root. All
imports resolve to this checkout's built packages; no provider
credentials are needed.

```js
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { createMCPTools } from './packages/mcp/dist/index.js';
import { convertToModelMessages } from './packages/ai/dist/index.js';

const { definitions, first } = JSON.parse(await readFile('/tmp/ai-sdk-webmcp-native-result.json', 'utf8'));
const tools = createMCPTools(definitions.tools);
const messages = await convertToModelMessages([
  {
    role: 'assistant',
    parts: [{
      type: 'dynamic-tool',
      toolName: 'getCart',
      toolCallId: 'call-1',
      state: 'output-available',
      input: {},
      output: first,
    }],
  },
], { tools });
const result = messages.find(message => message.role === 'tool').content[0];
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  tools: Object.keys(tools),
  hasExecute: typeof tools.getCart.execute === 'function',
  result,
}));
if (tools.getCart.execute != null || result.output.type !== 'content' || !result.output.value[0].text.includes('75')) {
  process.exitCode = 1;
}
```

Observed: `tools: ["getCart"]`, `hasExecute: false`, and a `tool-result`
containing text `{"quantity":3,"total":75}`.

</details>



<details>
<summary>Review regression: invalid MCP App URI metadata</summary>

Save the following script as `/tmp/ai-sdk-mcp-app-uri-repro.mjs`. Before
and after the fix, build this checkout's MCP package and run the same
HTTP reproduction from the repository root:

```sh
pnpm exec turbo build --filter=@ai-sdk/mcp...
node --input-type=module < /tmp/ai-sdk-mcp-app-uri-repro.mjs
```

Both `_meta.ui.resourceUri` and legacy `_meta['ui/resourceUri']` with an
`https://` URI returned **HTTP 500** with a plain `Error` before the
fix. Both return **HTTP 400** (`Invalid browser tool definitions`)
afterward, using the documented `MCPClientError.isInstance` route
handler. No model credentials or browser are needed: invalid definitions
fail before model inference.

```js
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { createMCPTools, MCPClientError } from './packages/mcp/dist/index.js';

async function POST(request) {
  const { tools: definitions } = await request.json();
  try {
    createMCPTools(definitions);
  } catch (error) {
    if (!MCPClientError.isInstance(error)) throw error;
    return new Response('Invalid browser tool definitions', { status: 400 });
  }
  return new Response('OK');
}
const server = createServer(async (request, response) => {
  let body = '';
  for await (const chunk of request) body += chunk;
  let result;
  try {
    result = await POST(new Request('http://localhost/', { method: 'POST', body }));
  } catch (error) {
    result = new Response(`${error.name}: ${error.message}`, { status: 500 });
  }
  response.writeHead(result.status);
  response.end(await result.text());
});
await new Promise(resolve => server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', resolve));
try {
  for (const _meta of [
    { ui: { resourceUri: 'https://example.com/cart' } },
    { 'ui/resourceUri': 'https://example.com/cart' },
  ]) {
    const result = await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`, {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ tools: [{ name: 'getCart', inputSchema: {}, _meta }] }),
    });
    console.log(JSON.stringify({ _meta, status: result.status, body: await result.text() }));
  }
} finally {
  await new Promise(resolve => server.close(resolve));
}
```

</details>

